Upstream's generate_engine_version() shells out to `git rev-parse` to
embed a short commit sha in the build's ENGINE_VERSION string. Gentoo
builds from a release tarball (SRC_URI), which has no .git directory,
so this lookup always fails and .filter()/.and_then() silently discard
the error -- it's tolerated, not fixed. The practical effect is a
useless `git` fork on every single build.

Skip the lookup outright instead of tolerating a failure that will
never succeed in this build environment.

Not submitted upstream: this is a Gentoo packaging-specific build
environment difference (no .git in the release tarball), not a bug
in the upstream code path itself.
--- a/build/build.rs
+++ b/build/build.rs
@@ -126,13 +126,11 @@
 fn generate_engine_version() {
     let version = env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION");

-    let git_sha = Command::new("git")
-        .args(["rev-parse", "--short=8", "HEAD"])
-        .output()
-        .ok()
-        .filter(|v| v.status.success())
-        .and_then(|v| String::from_utf8(v.stdout).ok())
-        .map(|v| v.trim().to_string());
+    // Gentoo: release tarballs have no .git directory, so the upstream
+    // `git rev-parse` lookup below always fails and needlessly forks
+    // `git` on every build. Skip it outright instead of tolerating
+    // the failure.
+    let git_sha: Option<String> = None;

     if let Some(sha) = git_sha {
         println!("cargo:rustc-env=ENGINE_VERSION={version}-{sha}")
